WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:07.010
Glass started out life as a 150-page script,
which is M Night Shyamalan's longest screenplay

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:08.010
to date.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:11.440
And he did film that whole script, which gave
him a first cut of three hours and twenty

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.440
minutes.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:16.040
Over an hour of footage from that early cut
was deleted as the movie was edited down to

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:18.080
around two hours and ten minutes.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.039
Given that Shyamalan financed much of the
$20 million budget of Glass himself, it's

00:00:22.039 --> 00:00:25.869
understandable that he wishes he'd been a
bit 'harsher' during the writing process and

00:00:25.869 --> 00:00:28.189
pared down the script before shooting began.

00:00:28.189 --> 00:00:31.609
Still, that means we've got some interesting
deleted scenes to talk about!

00:00:31.609 --> 00:00:35.910
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm going to reveal and explain

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:40.440
seven intriguing scenes that were deleted
from Glass including an alternate ending.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.500
Spoilers ahead if you haven't seen the film
yet.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:47.100
One of the most striking deleted scenes from
Glass takes place in the corridors of the

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.730
psychiatric hospital.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:52.920
In the cut scene, we see several of the hospital's
other patients approaching the Beast, and,

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:58.019
as they do so, he seems to be laying hands
on them, as if they're having an almost religious

00:00:58.019 --> 00:00:59.019
experience.

00:00:59.019 --> 00:01:02.360
The Beast believes that only those who've
suffered are pure, so it's natural that he

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:06.530
would see the other patients at the hospital
as his followers.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:10.460
Shyamalan has said he chose a mustard colour
for the Beast's clothing because it's associated

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:14.520
with religious ceremonies and he regards the
Beast as "an evangelist, a preacher who wants

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.140
to help save The Broken."

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:20.830
And remember that a number of Kevin's personalities,
including Patricia and Dennis, talk about

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:25.470
the Beast in religious terms, for example,
referencing his prey as "sacred food".

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:30.600
Dr Staple even refers to Patricia as a kind
of 'high priestess' and when doubt about the

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.200
Beast finally creeps in, Patricia says that
her 'horde is losing faith'.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.630
It would seem logical, therefore, if the full
deleted scene also showed us the Beast preaching

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.490
and converting the patients to his cause.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:47.130
In the final film, we see David Dunn, aka
the Overseer, demonstrate his superpowers

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:51.430
just once before the story moves on to him
tracking down and battling the Beast.

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:55.870
That scene in the movie takes place in the
home of two men who randomly attacked a passer-by

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:59.510
on the street then uploaded a video of the
attack to the internet.

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:03.960
However, another scene of David's crime-fighting
exploits in the city was deleted from the

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:04.960
movie.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:08.390
In this trailer footage that didn't make it
into the final film, you can see the Overseer

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:12.160
slamming someone up against what looks like
it could be a subway wall.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:15.640
If you look carefully at the clip, you can
also see someone lying on the floor with the

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.180
contents of their handbag or purse spilled
out on the ground beside them.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:23.140
All of which points to the person on the floor
having been assaulted or mugged by the guy

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.900
being thrown against the wall by the Overseer.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:30.420
This scene looks like it's part of a longer
introduction to Dunn's vigilante activities,

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.550
and so I expect it was cut to get to the main
action of Dunn versus the Beast more quickly

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:37.380
and also trim down the movie's run-time.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:41.810
Shyamalan made good use in Glass of several
deleted scenes from Unbreakable including

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:46.150
one with young Elijah at a fair and another
with Joseph telling David that he knows his

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:47.770
secret identity.

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:51.620
But those weren't the only deleted scenes
from the nearly 20-year-old film that the

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:55.100
director was going to include in the final
part of his trilogy.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:59.510
Originally, a deleted Unbreakable scene where
David speaks to a priest was also going to

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:01.150
feature in Glass.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:05.370
In the scene, which takes place in a church,
David tells a priest that he feels the fact

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:10.330
he was the sole survivor of the train wreck
means something important, however, the priest,

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:14.770
who also lost family in the crash, tells David
in no uncertain terms that his survival is

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:17.250
no "hand of God, miracle" moment.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:18.850
"You were chosen?"

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:20.990
"I don't think so."

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:24.840
Shyamalan has said he decided to cut this
powerful scene from Glass at the last moment.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.990
I think the scene probably was going to appear
just after the group therapy session, when

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:32.580
we see David Dunn sitting alone in his room,
looking despondent.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:37.020
It would have fit perfectly there as David
is clearly shaken by Dr Staple's words about

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:41.540
how all his supposed superpowers are just
a matter of intuition and tricks.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:45.490
So, it makes sense that he might think back
in that moment to someone else from his past

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:48.530
– the priest – who likewise doubted that
he was special.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.430
"It was luck."

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.970
"What do you mean?"

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:54.880
"Random, without meaning."

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:59.290
Actor James McAvoy also had an extended scene
with the cheerleaders at the beginning of

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:00.290
the movie.

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:04.709
The final film starts with Dennis and Patricia
off-screen, discussing people's lack of belief

00:04:04.709 --> 00:04:09.160
in the Beast; then we see Patricia introduce
herself to the four abducted girls.

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:13.560
"Now, who'd like a PB and J sandwich?"

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:18.079
However, according to Shyamalan, in the original
script, the opening sequence with Patricia

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:19.280
was much longer.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:23.830
The director thought he needed to explain
how Kevin had multiple personalities, and

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:27.539
so the cheerleaders were going to go through
a similar set of scenes like Casey and the

00:04:27.539 --> 00:04:30.550
two girls did when they were first abducted
in Split.

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:34.710
In the end though, Shyamalan realised he was
just repeating things people had already seen

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:39.400
in that film and that he didn't need the extra
scenes in Glass to properly re-introduce the

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.150
audience to Kevin Wendell Crumb and his various
identities.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:46.850
Kevin has a total of 24 personalities including
The Beast.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:51.569
In Split, we saw eight of those identities
on display either in person or via the video

00:04:51.569 --> 00:04:53.779
recordings on Kevin's computer.

00:04:53.779 --> 00:04:58.539
But while Glass showed us many more personalities,
James McAvoy has confirmed that although he

00:04:58.539 --> 00:05:03.490
filmed 23 of Kevin's identities, only 20 of
them made the final cut of the film.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:07.460
I've compared the list of characters that
McAvoy's credited for in Glass against the

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:11.259
full list of Kevin's identities revealed on
his computer in Split.

00:05:11.259 --> 00:05:15.870
It looks like the missing characters in Glass
are Bernice, Rakel, and Ansel, as well as

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:18.710
Orwell, who we did see a clip of in Split.

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:23.909
Shyamalan has said that because he was originally
going to include 23 out of Kevin's 24 identities

00:05:23.909 --> 00:05:27.710
in Glass, at one point he "was going to do
a contest to tell [him] which character is

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:28.840
not in the movie".

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:33.190
Anyway, I'm hoping on the Blu-ray we get to
see an extended scene of Dr Staple meeting

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:38.050
Kevin's multiple identities which shows us
the remaining personalities not seen in either

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:39.050
film.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:43.080
There's also an interesting little moment
with David in the group therapy session that

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:46.089
ended up being scrapped:
"So you're not going to shake my hand, and

00:05:46.089 --> 00:05:47.580
let me walk on out of here."

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:51.830
He's talking here to Dr Staple, and had this
clip been in the final film, it would have

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:56.339
been a chilling bit of foreshadowing of how,
towards the end of the movie, Dr Staple holds

00:05:56.339 --> 00:06:00.939
David's hand and watches on while he's drowned
in a puddle by one of the secret society's

00:06:00.939 --> 00:06:01.939
henchmen.

00:06:01.939 --> 00:06:05.879
I wonder if the ultimate decision to scrap
this brief clip was partly because Shyamalan

00:06:05.879 --> 00:06:10.099
felt it tipped its hat a little too early
to the fact that David Dunn would find out

00:06:10.099 --> 00:06:14.150
something sinister about Dr Staple by touching
her hand.

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:18.839
The finale of Glass has proved divisive among
fans, but Samuel L Jackson has revealed that

00:06:18.839 --> 00:06:21.259
initially the movie had a different ending.

00:06:21.259 --> 00:06:25.349
In an interview with Digital Spy, he explained
that the original ending was "changed because

00:06:25.349 --> 00:06:29.319
of the way society is and what's going on
in the world and what it would have looked

00:06:29.319 --> 00:06:30.319
like."

00:06:30.319 --> 00:06:33.849
Jackson's comments are rather vague, but one
possibility is that originally the black-clover

00:06:33.849 --> 00:06:39.650
secret society suppressing super-powered individuals
was meant to be a covert agency of the government,

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:43.240
rather than a group whose affiliations are
never clearly stated.

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:47.449
When Dr Staple's henchmen arrive, they're
dressed and labelled like regular law enforcement

00:06:47.449 --> 00:06:49.300
officials or SWAT teams.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:52.860
Given that Glass is set in the present, if
the twist was going to be that it was the

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:57.039
government that was secretly repressing and
assassinating supers, then it might have felt

00:06:57.039 --> 00:07:00.449
the movie was trying to make a deliberate
political point.

00:07:00.449 --> 00:07:04.319
Another possibility is that the final twist
was changed as it ended the movie on an even

00:07:04.319 --> 00:07:08.360
darker note, which was also the reason that
Shyamalan changed the original ending of his

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:09.879
previous movie Split.

00:07:09.879 --> 00:07:14.669
Of course, the three main characters are killed
off in Glass, but what if after Elijah's mother,

00:07:14.669 --> 00:07:18.879
Joseph and Casey released the footage to the
world, it ended up being dismissed as "fake

00:07:18.879 --> 00:07:21.080
news" by the government or the media?

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:25.440
During the final scene, we hear Mr Glass say,
"Whoever these people are who don't want us

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:27.469
to know the truth, today they lose."

00:07:27.469 --> 00:07:31.499
But I wonder if an earlier draft saw the government
discrediting the footage and covering up the

00:07:31.499 --> 00:07:32.499
whole affair.

00:07:32.499 --> 00:07:35.629
I don't know if Shyamalan had anything like
that in mind, but it would have been a pretty

00:07:35.629 --> 00:07:40.479
depressing ending and again given the movie
a more overtly political ending.

00:07:40.479 --> 00:07:44.569
Apparently, also at a Q&amp;A event for Glass,
Shyamalan talked about how the distributors

00:07:44.569 --> 00:07:49.499
Disney and Universal offered him a larger
budget to make a more climactic ending.

00:07:49.499 --> 00:07:54.720
It's possible that their idea was to actually
have that big show-down at the Osaka Tower.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:57.969
Shyamalan turned down the offer, however,
as he says he's not as comfortable making

00:07:57.969 --> 00:08:02.229
films with huge budgets and feels he works
better with smaller ones, so he kept this

00:08:02.229 --> 00:08:08.770
film down to $20 million overall and its CGI
budget was only $800,000, a tiny amount for

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:09.770
a superhero movie.

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:14.059
Either way, if you want to know more about
the actual ending in the movie and who the

00:08:14.059 --> 00:08:18.800
black-clover secret society really represent,
then tap here to watch my Ending Explained

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:21.629
